.TH DVBTX 7 2026-11-08 "dvbtx 0.3.1" "dvbtx manual"
.SH NAME
dvbtx \- how the dvbtx DVB transmitter works: components, data flow, timing, failures
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dvbtx
builds complete DVB multiplexes from live streams, capture devices, files or
pipes and modulates them on software radios (HackRF One, USRP B200/B210, any
SoapySDR transmitter).
Any number of radios, of carriers (multiplexes) per radio and of services
(channels) per carrier are described in one TOML file
.RB ( dvbtx.toml (5))
or on the command line
.RB ( dvbtx (1)).
All the carriers of a radio are synthesised together at the radio's sample
rate, in any mix of DVB\-T, DVB\-T2, DVB\-C, DVB\-S and DVB\-S2.
.PP
The system is made of separate processes:
.IP \(bu 2
the
.B supervisor
.RB ( "dvbtx run" ,
Python): configuration, frequency plan, SI tables, EPG, alerts, supervision
of the other processes, web page;
.IP \(bu
one
.B ingest
process (ffmpeg, possibly fed by streamlink or a command) per source, turning
it into a single-program MPEG\-TS on a local UDP port;
.IP \(bu
the
.B engine
.RB ( dvbtx\-engine (8),
C): UDP reception, multiplexing, channel coding, synthesis and radio output,
clocked by the radios;
.IP \(bu
optionally one
.B TSDuck
analyser
.RB ( tsp (1))
per multiplex.
.PP
A source that crashes, freezes or lags is only a process that stops writing to
a UDP port: it never disturbs the other services, its own service plays its
backup and comes back on its own.
.SH LEGAL WARNING
.B Closed coax only.
Transmitting on broadcast television frequencies without a licence is illegal.
dvbtx is meant for a private coaxial network or a direct cable to the TV set,
with attenuators, never for an antenna.
.SH DATA FLOW
.EX
 sources             ingest: one process     dvbtx-engine
                     per source
 HLS, HTTP, M3U --+
 YouTube, Twitch -+  [streamlink|yt-dlp]
 RTMP, RTSP, SRT -+-> ffmpeg -c copy or  --> UDP 127.0.0.1:base_port+id
 tvheadend (HTSP) +   encode (CPU/GPU)          |
 v4l2, screen ----+   restarted on failure      v
 files, dir: -----+                      input: PAT/PMT demux,
 pipe:, stdin:, --+                      ideal departure time per
 exec:                                   packet (de-jitter buffer)
 udp+direct:// ------------------------->       |
                                                v
   supervisor: NIT, SDT, EIT --------->  multiplexer per carrier,
   section files, TOT descriptor         pulled by its modulator:
                                         SI > due packet > PCR > null
                                         PCR restamp, PID remap,
                                         PAT/PMT/TDT/TOT, backup switch
                                                |
                                                v
                                         channel coding per carrier:
                                         DVB-T, T2, C, S, S2
                                                |
                                                v
                                         synthesis per radio: one IFFT
                                         (DVB-T), filter banks, T2 groups
                                                |
                                                v
                                         plugin: hackrf, uhd, soapy,
                                         null  -->  radio  -->  coax
 copy of each multiplex:
 udp://127.0.0.1:monitor_base_port+n --> TSDuck tsp, web previews,
                                         carrier.monitor addresses
 engine stdout: statistics (JSON) ----> supervisor --> web, alerts
 engine stdin: commands <-------------- supervisor
.EE
.SH COMPONENTS
.SS Supervisor
.B dvbtx run
(normally the
.B dvbtx
systemd service) reads the configuration, validates it and computes the plan
.RB ( "dvbtx check"
shows it without transmitting), writes the engine's INI file
.RI ( work_dir /engine.ini ,
see
.BR dvbtx\-engine (8))
and the SI section files, starts the engine, the web page, the ingest
processes and the analysers, then runs a loop once a second.
.TP
.B Configuration
TOML file
.RI ( /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml )
and/or command line, with
.B \-s
.IB key = value
overrides.
.B [[radio]]
sections hold
.B [[carrier]]
multiplexes (standard, frequency or UHF/VHF channel, modulation, TSID) which
hold
.B [[carrier.service]]
channels (name, LCN, source or sources, backup, EPG id, encoding).
Channels can be added, removed, renamed, renumbered or given another source
while on the air
.RB ( dvbtx\-channel (1),
the web page,
.BR "systemctl reload dvbtx" );
only that channel is touched, through engine commands, and the change is written
back to the file.
A change of radios or multiplexes restarts the engine on the new plan (a cut of
a second or two; unchanged sources keep running).
.TP
.B Frequency plan
Unless forced, the supervisor computes each radio's centre frequency and
sample rate.
One carrier: the centre is put just outside the occupied band (1.05 times its
half width below the carrier), so that the local oscillator leakage stays out
of the channel; several carriers: the middle of the band.
DVB\-T carriers must share mode, guard interval and bandwidth on one radio, and
sit on its subcarrier grid; the sample rate is then a whole multiple of the
subcarrier spacing (one FFT size for all).
For DVB\-T2 the planner prefers a sample rate that is a whole multiple of the
T2 elementary rate, with the centre on the P1 subcarrier grid; for DVB\-C, \-S,
\-S2 a rate of which the symbol rates are simple fractions.
For a HackRF the analog filter is chosen to cover the band edges.
Carriers closer than 50 kHz are refused, and so is a plan needing more than
the driver can do (HackRF 8\(en20 MS/s, UHD 0.2\(en61.44 MS/s).
service_id (TSID\(mu256 + the position of the service, from 1), LCN (the next free number from 1), PIDs (0x100 +
n\(mu0x20 per service) and the service type (from the streams the engine sees)
are computed unless given.
.TP
.B SI tables
The supervisor builds, as binary section files in
.IR work_dir /si/,
the NIT (network name, delivery system descriptor of each multiplex, EACEM
private_data_specifier and logical_channel_descriptor 0x83 for the LCNs), the
SDT (actual, and other with
.BR network.sdt_other ),
the EIT present/following and schedule, and the local_time_offset_descriptor
of the TOT (country and time zone).
Version numbers change only when a table's content does.
The engine carousels them (NIT every 2 s, SDT 1 s, EIT p/f 1 s, EIT schedule
10 s by default) and generates the PAT, PMT, TDT and TOT itself; it re-reads a
file within a second when it changes.
Many TVs ignore the LCNs when the original_network_id is not their country's:
.B onid
and
.B network_id
default to 0x20FA (France).
Text is ISO 8859\-9 with its prefix byte by default
.RB ( network.charset ).
.TP
.B EPG
Every XMLTV or TSDuck EIT XML file in
.B epg.dir
.RI ( /var/lib/dvbtx/epg/
in the packaged configuration) is loaded, and XMLTV URLs
.RB ( epg.urls )
are fetched every
.B epg.url_refresh_s
(3600 s).
Events are matched to services by
.BR epg_id ,
display name or service_id, and become EIT present/following (rewritten at
each event boundary) and schedule
.RB ( epg.days ,
7 days, rebuilt every 10 minutes and on
.BR SIGHUP ),
with genre and parental rating.
.B dvbtx epg
shows what each service gets.
.TP
.B Alerts
Evaluated every 2 s from the engine statistics: engine not running (no
statistics for 10 s), input demand above the channel capacity, multiplex fuller
than
.B alerts.overload_percent
(98 %), decoding margin below
.B alerts.margin_min_ms
(150 ms; critical below zero), late packets dropped or later than
.B alerts.late_ms
(300 ms), service on its backup or on a fallback source, no source, input
faster than real time, radio not found or lost, radio underruns or gaps the
radio reports, ingest process failing repeatedly, TSDuck transport errors.
They go to the log, the web page, a JSON POST to
.BR alerts.webhook ,
and
.BR alerts.command .
.TP
.B Supervision
Each second the supervisor restarts the engine if it exited, follows the
radios, polls the ingest processes, restarts stalled or discontinuous
sources, switches sources of channels that have several, and keeps the
analysers and previews running (see
.BR FAILURES ).
.B systemctl reload dvbtx
.RB ( SIGHUP )
re-reads the EPG and the channels of the file.
.SS Ingest processes
One process per input: the primary source of each service, plus one per
distinct backup source (shared by the services that use the same one).
Each writes a single-program MPEG\-TS to
.BI udp://127.0.0.1: port
(1316-byte datagrams) with
.B \-muxdelay 0.7
and a PCR every 20 ms.
.IP \(bu 2
ffmpeg copies the streams
.RB ( "\-c copy" );
capture sources (v4l2, screen, lavfi...), sources whose codecs DVB receivers do
not decode, and services with
.B encode
are encoded, on a GPU when one works (Intel/AMD VAAPI or QSV, NVIDIA NVENC,
learnt once and again when the cards or drivers change), else with libx264;
.IP \(bu
HLS URLs go through
.B streamlink
when it is installed, which rides the stream discontinuities ffmpeg's HLS
reader stalls on;
.B twitch://
too;
.B ytdlp:
URLs are resolved by
.BR yt\-dlp ;
.B exec:
and
.B stdin:
feed ffmpeg's standard input;
.IP \(bu
.B udp+direct://
sources have no process: the engine receives them itself.
.PP
A process that exits is started again after
.B ingest.restart_min_s
(1 s), doubled at each failure up to
.B ingest.restart_max_s
(30 s), and back to the minimum once it has run for 60 s.
Every child dies with the supervisor.
The sources of a multiplex that is stopped, or whose radio is missing, are
paused.
See
.BR dvbtx\-sources (7).
.SS Engine
.BR dvbtx\-engine (8)
does the real-time work in C, with real-time thread priorities:
.TP
.B UDP reception
One thread receives every input, demultiplexes PAT and PMT and keeps the packets
of the selected program.
.TP
.B De-jitter buffer
Each packet gets an ideal departure time: the arrival time of the first PCR
plus a fixed buffer
.RB ( buffer_ms ),
then interpolated between PCRs, so that bursts and network jitter disappear.
The anchor is slewed by at most 30 ppm to keep the smallest margin of each
2-second window between a quarter and one and a half buffer, and reset after
three windows far out of it.
.B buffer_ms
is 6000 ms for sources delivered in segments (YouTube, Twitch, .m3u8) and 1000
ms otherwise unless set (100 to 30000).
.TP
.B Multiplexer
One per carrier, pulled packet by packet by its modulator at the exact
transport stream rate of the channel, so the rate is exact by construction.
Each slot gets, in that order: a due PSI/SI packet; the most urgent service
packet whose ideal time has come (earliest deadline first, the deadline moved
100 ms per priority step); a PCR-only packet when a service has had none for
.B pcr_max_ms
(35 ms); else a null packet.
PIDs are remapped per service, continuity counters rewritten, PAT and PMT
rebuilt.
.TP
.B PCR restamp
A PCR leaves with the source PCR plus the packet's lateness against its ideal
time: it matches the real emission time (measured jitter under 0.5 \(mcs).
.TP
.B Backup switch
A service whose primary input is down (nothing for
.B switch_timeout_ms
once its buffer has played out) switches to its backup input on a key frame,
and back when the primary has been up for
.BR return_ms .
A new source given to a running service replaces the old one on a video key
frame, without a cut.
.TP
.B Channel coding
DVB\-T (EN 300 744: 2k/8k, QPSK to 64\-QAM, every code rate and guard
interval), DVB\-T2 (EN 302 755, single PLP: 1K to 32K, normal and extended
carriers, PP1 to PP8, QPSK to 256\-QAM rotated, normal and short FEC frames;
a port of GNU Radio gr\-dtv), DVB\-C (J.83 annex A, 16 to 256\-QAM), DVB\-S
(QPSK, 1/2 to 7/8) and DVB\-S2 (QPSK, 8PSK, 16APSK, 32APSK, normal and short
frames, pilots) with DVB\-S2X (EN 302 307\-2: 8APSK to 256APSK, the S2X code
rates, roll-offs 0.15, 0.10 and 0.05; VL\-SNR excepted).
The DVB\-S2 coder works on words (BCH by carry-less multiplication when the
processor has PCLMUL, LDPC parity by 8 \(mu 8 bit transposes): about 24 cycles per
256APSK symbol.
A carrier of standard
.B ts
is only multiplexed, and sent to a UDP address or a file at its rate.
.TP
.B Single-FFT synthesis
All the DVB\-T carriers of a radio go through one IFFT per OFDM symbol, at the
radio rate: the FFT size is the radio rate divided by the subcarrier spacing,
each carrier placed at its offset.
.TP
.B Filter-bank synthesis
DVB\-C, DVB\-S, DVB\-S2 and DVB\-T2 carriers are synthesised directly at the
radio rate by frequency-domain filter banks (overlap-save): each block of
symbols goes through an FFT, is weighted by the exact root-raised-cosine
response (flat for the DVB\-T2 resampling), placed at the carrier frequency,
and one IFFT per bank gives the radio samples.
From 30 MS/s, worker threads compute several blocks at once
.RB ( synth_threads
in
.BR dvbtx\-engine (8)).
This needs symbol rates that are simple fractions of the radio rate; otherwise,
or with
.BR "synth = \(dqfir\(dq" ,
a 16-symbol polyphase filter is used.
When the radio rate is a whole multiple k of the DVB\-T2 rate, the T2 carriers
are not resampled at all: each OFDM symbol is one IFFT of k times the FFT size
at the radio rate, shared by the T2 carriers with the same FFT size, guard
interval and frame length.
The radio's power is shared between its carriers by
.BR gain_db ,
13 dB below full scale
.RB ( backoff_db ).
.TP
.B Radio plugins
.BR hackrf ,
.BR uhd ,
.B soapy
and
.B null
(no hardware, optionally writing the samples to a file), loaded at run time so
that only the libraries of the radios in use are needed.
.PP
The engine prints its statistics as one JSON line a second on its standard
output and takes commands on its standard input (add or remove inputs and
services, change a service's source, stop or start a carrier, add a copy of a
multiplex).
.SS TSDuck analysis
When
.B monitoring.tsduck
is
.B auto
(default) and
.BR tsp (1)
is installed, or
.BR true ,
one
.B "tsp \-I ip \-P continuity \-P analyze"
reads the copy of each multiplex on its monitor port and gives a JSON report
every
.B monitoring.interval
(5 s): bit rates per service and PID, continuity errors, transport errors.
The report is shown on the web page and sent to the
.B monitoring.export
addresses
.RB ( \-\-analysis\-export
.BR udp:// " or " tcp:// );
transport errors raise an alert.
.B monitoring.stats_export
.RB ( \-\-stats\-export )
receives the engine statistics and the active alerts every second.
.SS Web page
.B http://127.0.0.1:8080/
by default
.RB ( web.listen ):
radios, multiplexes, services, sources, alerts and logs, live; previews and the
live picture of each channel, the raw multiplex
.RI ( /api/mux/ n .ts );
channels, radios, multiplexes and network settings edited (validated, saved in
the file, applied on demand); Stop and Start per multiplex; a JSON API and
.I /metrics
for Prometheus.
It starts before the sources.
See
.BR dvbtx\-web (7).
.SS Power profiles
On battery,
.B dvbtx power
(opt-in:
.BR "systemctl enable dvbtx\-power" )
keeps the machine in a saving profile while nothing is on the air and switches
to a transmit profile while a dvbtx unit runs (called by the units at start and
stop, also after a crash).
With
.B engine.cpu_latency_us = 100
the cores may still sleep between two radio buffers, but only safely while the processor is
busy (with it mostly idle, a HackRF at 19.6 MS/s ran short of data): the default 0 is the safe one.
See
.BR dvbtx\-power (8).
.SH TIMING
Two clocks meet in the engine: the clock of the sources (their PCRs, mapped to
the system's monotonic clock by the de-jitter buffer) and the clock of each
radio (its sample counter).
.TP
.B Radio clock
For each radio the engine keeps the monotonic time at which sample 0 is on the
air.
It starts at the start time plus the radio's buffer
.RB ( buffer_ms ,
50 ms) and the engine's own ring of 32 symbols or blocks, then follows the
sample counter the radio reports: the smallest offset of each 1-second window,
taken as is during the first 4 seconds, then approached by an eighth of the
difference, at most 50 \(mcs per second (50 ppm).
Packet slot n of a carrier leaves at that time plus n packet durations.
.TP
.B Clock lead
The multiplexer fills slots ahead of the air by the radio buffer plus the
symbols in the rings (and, for DVB\-T2, a whole T2 frame: one to two frames
between multiplexing and air).
The statistics show it per carrier
.RB ( clock_lead_ms ).
A packet is sent when its ideal time is at most
.B early_ms
(100 ms) after its slot's emission time, and dropped when it is more than
.B max_late_ms
(600 ms) late.
.TP
.B Decoding margin
For every frame the engine measures the time between its emission and its
decoding time in the receiver: DTS minus the PCR at emission (minimum and mean
per second, video and audio).
It is about 700 ms normally (ffmpeg's
.BR \-muxdelay ),
lower when the multiplex is too full; below zero the TV freezes.
.SH FAILURES
.TP
.B Source down
Nothing received for
.B switch_timeout_ms
(3 s) once its buffer has played out: the service switches to its backup clip
.RB ( backup ;
the packaged configuration uses
.IR /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts ,
made by
.BR "dvbtx make\-backup" )
on a key frame, and comes back when the source has been up for
.B return_ms
(10 s).
Without a backup the service is empty meanwhile.
An ffmpeg that runs but delivers nothing for
.B ingest.stall_restart_s
(20 s) is restarted; one that sees
.B ingest.discontinuity_restart
(10) timestamp discontinuities within 30 s is started again beside the old one
and swapped in; one whose video moved to new PIDs (an inserted ad) is restarted
at once; a streamlink source that passes an HLS discontinuity gets a standby
copy started from the live edge, used if the first one stalls.
.TP
.B Several sources
With
.B source
a list, the next source is started when the one in use has delivered nothing for
.B failover_s
(10 s) or has not started within
.B source_start_s
(30 s), the channel playing its backup meanwhile; the failed one is stopped
first (IPTV accounts limited to one connection).
The preferred sources are tried again every
.B preferred_retry_s
(300 s) beside the one playing, and taken back without a cut once they deliver.
.TP
.B Unstable source
A source is also left when its channel fell back to the backup
.B unstable_switches
(3) times within
.B unstable_window_s
(600 s), or stayed on it
.BR failover_s " + " return_ms .
A source left is retried later each time (5, 10, 20 minutes... up to an hour)
and forgiven once it has held 30 minutes.
.TP
.B Radio missing or unplugged
A radio that cannot be opened at start, or that fails while on the air (a
write error, or no sample consumed for 5 s), takes only its own multiplexes off
the air: the other radios go on, its sources are paused and an alert is raised.
The supervisor looks for it every 15 s
.RB ( "dvbtx\-engine \-\-probe" )
and restarts the engine once it is back (a short cut of the other radios); a
radio lost but not found again within 120 s also gets the engine restarted,
once, in case the engine itself held a wedged device.
With no radio at all, dvbtx runs without transmitting and the web page stays.
.TP
.B Multiplexes stopped
Stop on the web page takes a multiplex off the air at once (it is still coded,
its sources are paused) and is kept in the file
.RB ( "enabled = false" ).
A radio whose multiplexes are all stopped is closed, and opened again when one
starts.
.TP
.B Engine crash
The supervisor starts the engine again after 2, 4, 8, 16, then 30 s; the
sources keep running meanwhile.
A change the engine refuses also restarts it from the plan.
The engine stops when the supervisor dies, so that nothing stays on the air
unsupervised; the systemd unit restarts the supervisor after 5 s
.RB ( Restart=on\-failure ).
.SH PORTS
.TP
.IB ingest.base_port " + " id
(20100 + id): UDP port on 127.0.0.1 of the ingest process of input
.IR id .
Input ids are taken below
.BR "ingest.monitor_base_port \- ingest.base_port"
(800 by default) and below 1024.
.TP
.IB ingest.monitor_base_port " + " n
(20900 + n): copy of multiplex
.I n
sent by the engine to 127.0.0.1, read by the TSDuck analyser.
Web previews ask the engine for further copies on free ports, only while
someone looks.
.TP
.B carrier.monitor
Further copies of a multiplex, to any
.BI udp:// host : port\fR.
.TP
.B web.listen
127.0.0.1:8080 by default.
.PP
Sources received directly
.RB ( udp+direct:// )
and in listen mode (RTMP, SRT, UDP) use the addresses given in their URL.
.SH STANDARDS AND RADIOS
.TP
DVB\-T
2k/8k, QPSK to 64\-QAM: received over the air; sample-exact against GNU Radio
gr\-dtv on every mode, constellation, code rate and guard interval.
.TP
DVB\-T2
Single PLP, 1K to 32K, extended carriers, PP1 to PP8, up to 256\-QAM rotated,
normal and short frames: received over the air (32K 256\-QAM 5/6, 50 Mbit/s,
error-free); sample-exact against gr\-dtv on 23 configurations.
Not supported: MISO, PAPR reduction, several PLPs, FEFs, T2\-Lite.
.TP
DVB\-C
16 to 256\-QAM: symbol-exact against the mini\-cmts J.83A encoder; received over
the air (256\-QAM error-free from a B210, 128\-QAM from a HackRF, which is the
default constellation on a HackRF).
.TP
DVB\-S
QPSK 1/2 to 7/8: demodulated by leandvb.
.TP
DVB\-S2
QPSK, 8PSK, 16APSK, 32APSK: symbol-exact against gr\-dtv; 8PSK 9/10 at
20 Msym/s decoded error-free by gr\-dvbs2rx.
.TP
DVB\-S2X
Every MODCOD symbol-exact against gr\-dtv; received over the air by the dvbrx
software receiver (8APSK to 256APSK, roll-offs 0.15 to 0.05, no error).
.TP
HackRF One
Validated, up to 20 MS/s: 2 adjacent 8 MHz multiplexes.
.TP
USRP B200/B210 (UHD)
Validated, 56 MHz: 6 adjacent 8 MHz multiplexes (190 Mbit/s of payload in
DVB\-T), or one DVB\-S2X carrier up to 52 Msym/s (roll-off 0.05, 54.6 MHz).
.TP
SoapySDR transmitters
Built, not tried.
.SH MEASUREMENTS
Radio output cabled to a receiver:
.IP \(bu 2
HackRF, 1 DVB\-T multiplex 64\-QAM 7/8: C/N 30.9 dB, BER 0.
.IP \(bu
HackRF, 2 DVB\-T multiplexes (482 and 490 MHz): both locked, C/N about 29 dB,
BER 0; radio thread 25 % of a core, 26 % per multiplex.
.IP \(bu
B210, 6 DVB\-T multiplexes (474 to 514 MHz): all locked, C/N 26 to 34 dB, BER
0; radio 38 % of a core, about 25 % per multiplex, no underrun.
.IP \(bu
DVB\-T2 32K 256\-QAM 5/6 (50.08 Mbit/s): HackRF C/N 31.7 dB, B210 40 dB, no
error.
.IP \(bu
DVB\-C 256\-QAM 6.9 Msym/s (50.87 Mbit/s): B210 C/N 41.5 dB, no error.
.IP \(bu
B210 to a B210 receiver (dvbrx, pilot-trained equaliser), 61.4 MS/s: DVB\-S2X
256APSK 135/180 at 52 Msym/s, roll-off 0.05, SNR 23.8 dB, 300.2 Mbit/s with no
error; DVB\-S2 QPSK 3/4 at 46, 50 and 52 Msym/s (roll-offs 0.20, 0.10, 0.05),
SNR 16.6 to 17.8 dB, no error once locked.
Beyond about 40 Msym/s the B210 analog chain leaves a linear distortion floor
near 18 dB, which the receiver's equaliser removes.
.IP \(bu
Received stream through TSDuck: no continuity error, PCRs in spec, NIT, SDT,
EIT and TOT correct; a TV set finds the channels with names, numbers, EPG, AAC
and E\-AC\-3 5.1 sound.
.PP
Synthesis, measured with an ideal receiver on the null radio (16-bit
samples): 85.6 dB of MER for a 256\-QAM DVB\-C carrier beside another with the
filter banks, against 21 dB with the former 16-symbol polyphase filter; DVB\-T2
resampling within \-110 dB of ideal; direct T2 synthesis 148 dB on the continual
pilots for one carrier, 145.5 dB for two adjacent ones.
The OFDM side lobes are not filtered (\-49 dB at the band edge): a DVB\-C
carrier beside a DVB\-T2 one measures 59 dB of MER instead of 85.
Engine CPU, T2 32K 256\-QAM 5/6: 0.42 core at 18.29 MS/s with direct synthesis
against 1.16 with the filter bank at 16.33 MS/s; two T2 and one DVB\-C
256\-QAM carriers: 1.06 cores at 27.43 MS/s against 2.68 at 24.18 MS/s.
DVB\-S2X 256APSK 135/180 at 52 Msym/s on a B210 (i3\-8100T): 2.17 cores in all,
of which the filter bank 1.14 (four threads), the carrier (multiplexer and
coder) 0.57, UHD 0.40 with 16360-byte USB frames (0.58 with UHD's default).
.PP
Power, Intel i3\-8100T, four multiplexes on two radios: 22.1 W with the deep
C-states blocked and the performance governor, 13.9 W with the transmit profile
and
.BR "cpu_latency_us = 100" ,
no underrun; idle 1.37 W, 0.62 W with the saving profile (processor package).
.PP
The few bad packets an Astrometa DVB\-T2 USB key reports (5 to 15 a minute)
come from the key: the sent and received streams aligned packet by packet show
no bit error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/dvbtx/dvbtx.toml
Configuration
.RI ( /etc/dvbtx/ name .toml
for the
.BI dvbtx@ name
units).
.TP
.I /run/dvbtx/
Work directory:
.I engine.ini
and
.I si/
(section files).
.TP
.I /var/lib/dvbtx/backup.ts
Backup clip of the packaged configuration (where
.B dvbtx make\-backup
writes by default).
